  'U.S. History I (to 1877)',
  'This foundational undergraduate History course surveys North American and U.S. history from Indigenous worlds and European colonization through the Civil War and Reconstruction. The curriculum provides deep coverage of Indigenous sovereignty, Atlantic worlds, slavery and race, political economy, gender and reform, settler colonialism, emancipation, Reconstruction, and primary-source research.',
  $ush1json${"slug": "u-s-history-i-to-1877", "title": "U.S. History I (to 1877)", "description": "This foundational undergraduate History course surveys North American and U.S. history from Indigenous worlds and European colonization through the Civil War and Reconstruction. The curriculum provides deep coverage of Indigenous sovereignty, Atlantic worlds, slavery and race, political economy, gender and reform, settler colonialism, emancipation, Reconstruction, and primary-source research.", "topics": [{"id": "133ba0d1-d112-411c-a7eb-e5935c1c0882", "type": "topic", "title": "Historical Thinking, Sources, and Early America as a Field", "description": "Students begin by learning how historians build arguments about early North America and the United States from contested sources, interpretations, and archives.", "children": [{"id": "7b34d6e2-940e-4e7c-8320-63235fd4f3ac", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical thinking", "children": [{"id": "d699e14c-4063-4934-96cb-1f61d4e9add9", "type": "concept", "title": "Sourcing, contextualization, corroboration, chronology, causation, contingency, and continuity/change"}, {"id": "ea7d48a0-3bb3-47d8-a69c-7baaca456701", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary versus secondary sources, archives, historiography, and interpretive debate"}, {"id": "1930c88d-5981-44ef-beb7-598675a4232a", "type": "concept", "title": "How national narratives, public memory, and textbooks shape what feels obvious"}], "description": "Students learn that history is an evidence-based argument about change over time, not a memorized list of events."}, {"id": "03a8fee0-5465-4bfa-91f9-14bfc6a0df1f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Field framing", "children": [{"id": "deb6c2ad-57da-4d38-94e5-0eccbeba59ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Early America, colonial America, United States to 1877, Atlantic worlds, borderlands, and continental history"}, {"id": "81f30856-7b22-4804-a822-d5ca350d0067", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous sovereignty, European empires, African diaspora, settler colonialism, and republicanism"}, {"id": "19ff5abb-fcb4-43bd-b1ae-cb7b4615d27d", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale from household and community to empire, nation, region, and world"}], "description": "The point is to see U.S. History I as North American, Atlantic, Indigenous, imperial, and global history."}, {"id": "55af8876-ffba-4a04-b782-725e635eee50", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research habits", "children": [{"id": "f229e62f-c6ac-4198-91b6-dbcc3a7c3638", "type": "concept", "title": "Document annotation, citation, evidence logs, source comparison, and Chicago-style notes"}, {"id": "8f120c48-01d0-4fb7-8453-a524d1f86896", "type": "concept", "title": "Digitized archives, OCR limits, metadata, and responsible source use"}, {"id": "f8ef6b85-0879-4956-b010-281a84db8d68", "type": "concept", "title": "Turning questions into arguments supported by evidence"}], "description": "Students practice the basic skills used throughout the course."}]}, {"id": "ac951e3d-c422-42f4-a1bd-a42a81c93bd2", "type": "topic", "title": "Indigenous North America Before and After Contact", "description": "This module centers Indigenous polities, economies, cultures, sovereignties, and adaptations before and after European arrival.", "children": [{"id": "da47ac68-3450-4f9a-a611-09b0700e89c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenous worlds", "children": [{"id": "5b0ff0f0-2df6-492a-8c6d-143ae7c8426a", "type": "concept", "title": "Diverse nations, confederacies, languages, economies, diplomacies, kinship systems, and spiritual practices"}, {"id": "9597234b-cb69-4441-8f3f-af1f078af9a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Mississippian worlds, Haudenosaunee, Powhatan, Wendat, Pueblo, Cherokee, Wampanoag, and many others"}, {"id": "a02a1d06-d47d-44e8-9066-d6c92de2580e", "type": "concept", "title": "Agriculture, trade, warfare, diplomacy, migration, and environmental knowledge"}], "description": "Students start with Native societies as historical actors in their own right."}, {"id": "a9439e09-419d-48fd-af74-8a2943714f58", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Contact and exchange", "children": [{"id": "bd630f81-f66b-4349-8dfb-54ae4895ed1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Columbian exchange, disease, trade goods, missions, alliances, captivity, and intermarriage"}, {"id": "56c6533c-8eff-4daa-8fc2-a031c231875f", "type": "concept", "title": "Spanish, French, Dutch, English, African, and Indigenous encounters"}, {"id": "f97cd26b-81fa-4baa-90ae-5319887d7dcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Epidemics, demographic change, cultural adaptation, and diplomatic strategy"}], "description": "The gist is to study contact as mutual transformation and violence, not a one-sided beginning."}, {"id": "7b5f23f9-60ad-4fda-8219-7bcc3f405984", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sovereignty and persistence", "children": [{"id": "1a76f60e-bce9-48d4-9677-2f01c37f7002", "type": "concept", "title": "Treaties, land claims, diplomacy, warfare, removal pressures, and legal status"}, {"id": "7869bb8f-bd5c-42ba-b81a-5bfa1ce03cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral tradition, archaeology, material culture, maps, colonial records, and Native-authored sources"}, {"id": "fd7b6979-d017-4b1d-82ad-4b6e1b65640c", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding disappearance narratives and recognizing continuing Native sovereignty"}], "description": "Students learn to track Indigenous survival, adaptation, and resistance across the entire course."}]}, {"id": "b2f67234-4b4b-44f8-bcf8-16a6bf8ca2d4", "type": "topic", "title": "European Colonization and Atlantic Worlds", "description": "Students study colonization as part of Atlantic, imperial, environmental, commercial, religious, and Indigenous histories.", "children": [{"id": "2d9b4eb4-f0b6-406b-920f-97878655ced4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Imperial projects", "children": [{"id": "6604b9f5-670a-4a19-8e1a-06ad2a692f75", "type": "concept", "title": "Spanish, French, Dutch, English, Portuguese, and Indigenous imperial encounters"}, {"id": "2d80be4e-f6f8-498f-b6fc-a19290ad9a51", "type": "concept", "title": "Missions, trading posts, plantations, settler colonies, charter companies, and imperial wars"}, {"id": "35dc1221-c7ce-4652-ae43-5d18d816ba65", "type": "concept", "title": "Mercantilism, extraction, religion, labor, land, and sovereignty"}], "description": "Students compare colonization across empires and regions."}, {"id": "c027bd86-2748-4691-8f2e-49304f647500", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Atlantic systems", "children": [{"id": "5e710256-c64b-44f7-aeca-cd501876684b", "type": "concept", "title": "Triangular trade, migration, slavery, piracy, commodities, credit, and maritime labor"}, {"id": "92ab82d4-c9e5-4358-b551-f4c66265a0b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Caribbean, West Africa, Europe, Latin America, and North American ports"}, {"id": "c00261d1-e9be-48e1-acdb-635da0402873", "type": "concept", "title": "Sugar, tobacco, rice, fish, timber, rum, textiles, and silver"}], "description": "The point is to connect North America to the wider oceanic world."}, {"id": "d934e342-e352-46e5-893d-2331a68874ca", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environment and settlement", "children": [{"id": "8f598b89-1168-4ad8-b19d-679dcfba5e50", "type": "concept", "title": "Disease, crops, livestock, forests, fencing, waterways, and land-use change"}, {"id": "a81c29ff-887c-41a9-bd1e-b841f496ef41", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate, local ecology, regional economies, and settlement patterns"}, {"id": "2da57e47-6637-4f72-bd52-7e0de34ead61", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental consequences for Native communities, enslaved laborers, and settlers"}], "description": "Students examine colonization as ecological transformation."}]}, {"id": "1c2ebe16-a65b-465f-8db1-db2f07ede766", "type": "topic", "title": "Colonial Societies, Labor Systems, Religion, and Regional Diversity", "description": "This topic compares British North American colonies and neighboring imperial spaces through labor, race, religion, gender, class, and region.", "children": [{"id": "7a071505-8f28-4c8b-9267-af397b3bd8f9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional societies", "children": [{"id": "c5fe6d0c-b230-4b75-bc27-f5fccd9859b6", "type": "concept", "title": "New England, Chesapeake, Middle Colonies, Lower South, Caribbean, borderlands, and Native homelands"}, {"id": "b05b0d01-cb8e-4ed9-9ce0-cf6f3c396d9e", "type": "concept", "title": "Town, plantation, port city, frontier, mission, farm, and household economies"}, {"id": "610ea11d-0921-452b-ae0f-1de76707ec94", "type": "concept", "title": "Local governance, demography, disease, settlement density, and social hierarchy"}], "description": "Students compare colonies rather than treating colonial America as one place."}, {"id": "db349f09-bbb5-49cf-8b37-89f40f35c9bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor systems", "children": [{"id": "220a2aac-dd11-4237-a003-947eabde842e", "type": "concept", "title": "Indentured servitude, slavery, apprenticeship, wage labor, household labor, convict labor, and Native labor"}, {"id": "b2c704c5-ccd5-4980-b914-74450a20a929", "type": "concept", "title": "Race-making, legal status, gendered labor, skill, resistance, and mobility"}, {"id": "3ac85da6-dfd3-441e-a6f8-5e313969cb80", "type": "concept", "title": "Plantation economies, small farms, artisan production, and maritime labor"}], "description": "The gist is to connect work, coercion, status, and law."}, {"id": "95bb1c53-7d0d-4497-8a40-560c8a09ae74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and culture", "children": [{"id": "18b88d8d-7f56-4080-a99b-ba430cddbe63", "type": "concept", "title": "Puritanism, Anglicanism, Quakers, Catholicism, Judaism, African religions, Indigenous spirituality, and syncretism"}, {"id": "d545153b-e9d8-42de-a4e4-ffe354a8a1d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Great Awakening, dissent, literacy, print culture, missions, and education"}, {"id": "efe76515-2ea6-4aaa-ac03-caf20a410f3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious liberty, authority, community discipline, and pluralism"}], "description": "Students study belief as social force and political language."}]}, {"id": "78a6e20a-f698-4bad-8293-6c06f56c3946", "type": "topic", "title": "Slavery, Race, Resistance, and the Making of American Freedom", "description": "Students examine slavery and race as central to colonial and revolutionary America, not as side topics.", "children": [{"id": "823d0f41-674f-475b-a99d-77f2b0081541", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Slavery and capitalism", "children": [{"id": "9085fddc-8223-43d5-b3cb-02f58ad00114", "type": "concept", "title": "Atlantic slave trade, Middle Passage, plantation slavery, urban slavery, domestic trade, and commodity production"}, {"id": "6d29f101-3733-40cd-a9c2-818dae412fc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Tobacco, rice, indigo, sugar, cotton, finance, insurance, and trade networks"}, {"id": "814c9880-db32-45e1-bf5c-f0f2fab625a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Unfree labor and the contradictions of liberty"}], "description": "Students learn how slavery shaped wealth, labor, law, and political ideology."}, {"id": "3c26bcf6-2853-413a-a87d-e0ac840088ea", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and law", "children": [{"id": "25154de6-83f5-49bf-864f-01e08414591a", "type": "concept", "title": "Slave codes, hereditary status, partus sequitur ventrem, policing, punishment, and surveillance"}, {"id": "d331d047-2b08-44da-84f5-8a2902e4858a", "type": "concept", "title": "Whiteness, Blackness, Indigenous status, mixed ancestry, and freedom suits"}, {"id": "2c2b9d2b-3b4a-4202-82e1-25f74b1e104f", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship, property, personhood, family separation, and legal violence"}], "description": "The point is to show race as historically produced and legally enforced."}, {"id": "beed396b-221a-4f7a-83bd-6a576d04c274", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resistance and survival", "children": [{"id": "ebb25f4b-b05b-4c64-aacb-91309a8f7c21", "type": "concept", "title": "Maroonage, rebellion, everyday resistance, kinship, religion, literacy, escape, and negotiation"}, {"id": "39bb7d94-d935-4db0-aec4-2fc3eb3c770a", "type": "concept", "title": "Stono, Gabriel, Denmark Vesey, Nat Turner, and other resistance traditions"}, {"id": "bc654387-456f-4b27-8b34-3bd6a2ecc4c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Spirituals, community formation, memory, and survival strategies"}], "description": "Students center enslaved and free Black agency."}]}, {"id": "b3d1c29f-5d9e-426b-ad8e-40eb6998248c", "type": "topic", "title": "Imperial Crisis, American Revolution, and Founding Debates", "description": "This module studies the Revolution as a war, social crisis, political argument, and unfinished debate over rights and belonging.", "children": [{"id": "a38dc1ba-79a7-4e8c-81dc-14603c03fd0c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Imperial crisis", "children": [{"id": "f7febdb5-dc7f-496c-a108-9d68598a5f29", "type": "concept", "title": "French and Indian War, taxation, representation, boycotts, protests, pamphlets, and coercive acts"}, {"id": "62cb42ab-b539-4d69-8ac1-561d7bd9fce1", "type": "concept", "title": "Patriots, Loyalists, neutrals, Native nations, enslaved people, women, and imperial officials"}, {"id": "c6542559-7f71-4b83-9812-988dcb81044b", "type": "concept", "title": "Political economy, rights discourse, crowd action, and violence"}], "description": "Students trace how local disputes became imperial rupture."}, {"id": "b986611a-2cd5-43dd-afcc-5afa300acbf4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolutionary war", "children": [{"id": "5dea8272-e9de-4bfc-abeb-91a3f2618968", "type": "concept", "title": "Continental Army, militias, British strategy, French alliance, Native alliances, and Black military service"}, {"id": "1e597371-e736-483e-b4cb-64e4d730598f", "type": "concept", "title": "Home fronts, occupation, supply, disease, refugees, and loyalism"}, {"id": "1551d01c-14f9-4403-babd-78276aea86bb", "type": "concept", "title": "War aims, violence, emancipation claims, and independence"}], "description": "The gist is to see the Revolution as civil war, imperial war, and social struggle."}, {"id": "f627d5a3-dd62-4f1e-a3c1-7ad86d73d0e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Founding debates", "children": [{"id": "c01d0ed0-c5b1-4232-b96c-60fbefc6c8c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Declaration of Independence, Articles of Confederation, state constitutions, republicanism, and natural rights"}, {"id": "92c193e2-6b37-4fc5-a135-ef76f194cc81", "type": "concept", "title": "Slavery, women's rights, religious freedom, Native sovereignty, and property qualifications"}, {"id": "2b51d1f0-e26d-4a90-b9bb-6fd015e1c184", "type": "concept", "title": "Founding ideals versus founding exclusions"}], "description": "Students analyze contested meanings of liberty and equality."}]}, {"id": "5778d284-47cf-404b-97bc-5cef3d245dca", "type": "topic", "title": "Constitution, Early Republic, Political Economy, and Citizenship", "description": "Students trace the creation of federal institutions and the contested meanings of citizenship, sovereignty, liberty, and political economy.", "children": [{"id": "83635152-8b54-475a-b090-13179680a2b8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional order", "children": [{"id": "5f581d5d-9c26-449b-8524-493e5e49c9e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional Convention, federalism, separation of powers, Bill of Rights, ratification, and Anti-Federalists"}, {"id": "db04ae07-31f6-47b2-9910-9536b0c12610", "type": "concept", "title": "Executive power, judiciary, taxation, army, commerce, and representation"}, {"id": "9e7b7981-2ead-4c00-b3da-212ea7dc1594", "type": "concept", "title": "Compromise, slavery, sovereignty, and democratic legitimacy"}], "description": "Students learn how the new federal system was built and contested."}, {"id": "5ebd0197-774d-4400-ba1a-1a7daff67412", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early republic politics", "children": [{"id": "d3995340-8664-4744-a2b0-8493a3031bed", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalists, Democratic-Republicans, parties, elections, newspapers, public opinion, and political culture"}, {"id": "5b9ad8a1-3a93-404b-8268-24ffdc3b0c68", "type": "concept", "title": "Washington, Adams, Jefferson, Madison, Hamilton, and political economy"}, {"id": "f770d9ce-9969-446f-8992-76bad1007a17", "type": "concept", "title": "Whiskey Rebellion, Alien and Sedition Acts, Louisiana Purchase, War of 1812, and judicial review"}], "description": "The point is to connect institutions to political conflict."}, {"id": "76fff308-36ca-4d78-b809-14298a720201", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Citizenship and belonging", "children": [{"id": "3473c652-fe9c-43d4-9089-eb65d595f075", "type": "concept", "title": "Naturalization, voting rights, property, gender, race, slavery, Native nations, and free Black communities"}, {"id": "2669a326-33f0-4b76-994a-9e6288a1d095", "type": "concept", "title": "Republican motherhood, education, military service, taxation, and civic participation"}, {"id": "3c255843-3770-4b0d-a628-ea528dc9ba28", "type": "concept", "title": "Expansion of white male suffrage and exclusions from democracy"}], "description": "Students examine who counted as part of the republic."}]}, {"id": "bcfaf810-1b9c-4568-940b-a6758ee3a155", "type": "topic", "title": "Native Sovereignty, Settler Colonialism, and Westward Expansion", "description": "This topic follows expansion as conquest, migration, land hunger, treaty-making, resistance, and nation-building.", "children": [{"id": "191ee3b8-3668-4978-9134-15b61b0a53c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Settler colonialism", "children": [{"id": "ba7b4b08-55d8-40cc-a657-c911afdc673b", "type": "concept", "title": "Land hunger, surveys, speculation, treaties, squatting, military force, and federal policy"}, {"id": "817f0b1f-6a59-474b-9992-355ffca3caaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Northwest Territory, trans-Appalachian West, Louisiana Purchase, Texas, Oregon, California, and borderlands"}, {"id": "914dd9fc-a44e-470a-847d-712d056a33a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Settler ideology, racial formation, law, and violence"}], "description": "Students understand expansion as a structure of land acquisition and dispossession."}, {"id": "eefb5f8a-2d4d-405a-b5ec-37b8209fa1e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Native resistance and diplomacy", "children": [{"id": "25638801-14b5-406e-8e67-19622edec47e", "type": "concept", "title": "Tecumseh, Tenskwatawa, Creek War, Seminole resistance, Cherokee legal strategies, and Plains diplomacy"}, {"id": "2d6be8fd-c939-472e-9072-7c4d7f25446b", "type": "concept", "title": "Treaties, removal, missions, trade, war, adaptation, and pan-Indigenous movements"}, {"id": "89d768aa-0466-46e2-aaa8-c08b43a14145", "type": "concept", "title": "Sovereignty, nationhood, kinship, and survival"}], "description": "The gist is to follow Indigenous politics throughout U.S. expansion."}, {"id": "d147e81a-4569-47aa-9e53-c7030c5f7155", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Manifest Destiny and empire", "children": [{"id": "f8a0be63-2d12-4c31-8076-c4109370d827", "type": "concept", "title": "Mexican-American War, Indian Removal, Trail of Tears, gold rushes, military forts, and migration trails"}, {"id": "ca32c2d0-12dd-47db-bacb-cb4d40cf7b4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Borderlands, slavery expansion, race, citizenship, and conquest"}, {"id": "15e83fcc-f291-489a-b6af-e3204fb0edf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Maps, travel narratives, government records, and Native testimony"}], "description": "Students examine expansion as continental empire."}]}, {"id": "2ec5466b-dcbb-4105-b553-d23145446a9c", "type": "topic", "title": "Market Revolution, Jacksonian Democracy, Reform, and Everyday Life", "description": "Students examine economic transformation, democratic politics, religion, reform, family life, work, and social conflict.", "children": [{"id": "6aa0a153-0cae-4f25-9076-edfedb4e22d4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Market transformation", "children": [{"id": "b744b4bb-e85d-4b11-b9ad-9cac78c65bc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Canals, roads, steamboats, railroads, banks, factories, cotton, and commercial farming"}, {"id": "15ddda5a-724b-4a09-a723-2d56ef98e124", "type": "concept", "title": "Wage labor, artisans, clerks, textile workers, enslaved labor, and household production"}, {"id": "90cd048f-7dff-4ffe-972e-053a4ad3c8bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional integration, boom-bust cycles, class formation, and consumer culture"}], "description": "Students connect economic change to daily life and politics."}, {"id": "b8fd9d47-105a-497a-bfc2-2aa171c5f791", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Jacksonian politics", "children": [{"id": "cb297f8b-54da-44e7-a74e-06c148cd7b49", "type": "concept", "title": "White male suffrage, party organization, spoils system, Bank War, Indian Removal, and populist style"}, {"id": "e9546f8b-044b-4f32-a3fc-74c52e188f81", "type": "concept", "title": "Democrats, Whigs, newspapers, rallies, campaigns, and political participation"}, {"id": "96840280-1e66-4f99-832e-cae335f498d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Democracy for some, dispossession and disenfranchisement for others"}], "description": "The point is to see democratization and exclusion together."}, {"id": "4ee295ae-3d63-4575-86ee-3afd3acd614c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reform worlds", "children": [{"id": "01d69e4d-b816-4684-b1ea-52f9dd6d4bc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Second Great Awakening, temperance, prisons, asylums, education, women's rights, abolition, and utopian communities"}, {"id": "db8019b0-1c4d-42db-b08f-0c48dafae7a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral suasion, benevolent societies, print, voluntary associations, and lecture circuits"}, {"id": "e71d4ce0-8755-4fa5-985e-c6924db2866f", "type": "concept", "title": "Reform's radical possibilities and disciplinary impulses"}], "description": "Students examine reform as religious, moral, social, and political work."}]}, {"id": "1c62cef7-8c32-4310-80df-b2b9bf500902", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Family, Religion, Culture, and Print Publics", "description": "This module places cultural production, domestic life, religious movements, education, and print networks at the center of U.S. history.", "children": [{"id": "cd2e1e4a-3e4b-43e0-a9f6-32afdd6afc5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and family", "children": [{"id": "b8d277bf-4c71-45b7-ae88-d33532e2847b", "type": "concept", "title": "Republican motherhood, separate spheres, marriage, divorce, inheritance, sexuality, and domestic labor"}, {"id": "7256dd21-9391-4c12-a77d-9608359f4dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Women's education, paid work, reform activism, and legal constraints"}, {"id": "08744c32-a7da-4588-9d35-e5ef4ff0e2fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Family separation under slavery, Native kinship, and settler households"}], "description": "Students analyze private life as political and historical."}, {"id": "7469bc84-a5e5-4bbe-b772-372d499a5c87", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Culture and print", "children": [{"id": "c74d806e-46b3-428b-a4b8-a5875edeb683", "type": "concept", "title": "Newspapers, pamphlets, novels, slave narratives, almanacs, sermons, broadsides, and political cartoons"}, {"id": "8a215f26-bce6-4c72-8abd-d434af27b755", "type": "concept", "title": "Literacy, public sphere, postal networks, censorship, and persuasion"}, {"id": "572274da-a239-41fc-b098-88837e2d983b", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolitionist print, Native writing, women's writing, and popular culture"}], "description": "The gist is to see print and culture as engines of politics and identity."}, {"id": "8120fb3e-a3fa-4685-a896-4c91192e998e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and moral authority", "children": [{"id": "87bf202c-9c5a-4387-ae82-8cc430984cd4", "type": "concept", "title": "Revivals, evangelicalism, Catholic immigration, Judaism, African American churches, and Indigenous religious movements"}, {"id": "d6f54732-5038-461c-a6d0-a13c6b82fead", "type": "concept", "title": "Perfectionism, millennialism, moral reform, and social discipline"}, {"id": "3b6b8d1f-be93-4ddb-8868-5689d2ebe12a", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion in slavery, abolition, women's rights, temperance, and politics"}], "description": "Students connect religious experience to social change."}]}, {"id": "4380fedf-b6a4-4686-a954-c7fbcf3114b2", "type": "topic", "title": "Abolition, Antislavery, Proslavery, and Black Freedom Struggles", "description": "Students study the intellectual, political, religious, and grassroots battles over slavery and freedom.", "children": [{"id": "ed2d882d-7c48-48c2-ad80-423fcd1a45b8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Abolitionism", "children": [{"id": "67f4d8d4-2aaa-4c1a-ab38-fb2d5936bd88", "type": "concept", "title": "Immediate abolition, gradualism, colonization, moral suasion, political abolitionism, and direct action"}, {"id": "2d052507-696a-4933-b7e3-d1ccd2c053ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Garrison, Douglass, Tubman, Truth, Grimke sisters, Black conventions, vigilance committees, and free Black activism"}, {"id": "cf11965f-dac5-4535-960f-ec7404f6d120", "type": "concept", "title": "Petitions, newspapers, lectures, pamphlets, networks, and Underground Railroad"}], "description": "Students learn how abolition became a social movement and political force."}, {"id": "4ed6727d-2edd-47bd-814d-db3a830e9908", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Proslavery thought", "children": [{"id": "7b6aa3c1-b1f3-4e67-8dba-79e5c6b50f2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Paternalism, racial ideology, biblical defenses, states' rights, property rights, and political economy"}, {"id": "3c2f4dfd-c51f-4ef3-a46a-147c21fb881f", "type": "concept", "title": "Slaveholders, politicians, churches, newspapers, universities, and courts"}, {"id": "e7c2f129-c958-432f-aef7-0a9cd952facf", "type": "concept", "title": "Censorship, gag rule, violence, and expansionist arguments"}], "description": "The point is to understand how enslavers defended power and shaped politics."}, {"id": "94772bbc-78b8-43c8-b694-ce52f0f3da30", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Freedom struggles", "children": [{"id": "753bf338-e628-49a1-9bcd-9d3c49087af1", "type": "concept", "title": "Self-emancipation, family preservation, literacy, churches, lawsuits, military service, and community building"}, {"id": "ab392796-b53b-42bd-b89a-a464e7db1238", "type": "concept", "title": "Fugitive Slave Act, Dred Scott, personal liberty laws, and northern racism"}, {"id": "a00d4486-3153-4fec-b8a6-f0370bcccc86", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolition and Black freedom as national and transatlantic movements"}], "description": "Students center Black action in the long fight against slavery."}]}, {"id": "3690ab78-9228-44eb-ac0f-7c3e1f54d3e2", "type": "topic", "title": "Sectional Crisis, Compromise, and the Collapse of the Union", "description": "This topic explains why conflicts over slavery, territory, party politics, capitalism, and constitutional order became irreconcilable.", "children": [{"id": "7490a2f2-e7bf-48d5-a7aa-f9be19c08d2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Territorial conflict", "children": [{"id": "c4b4555b-dc2f-44e9-afeb-54f85a6b83b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Missouri Compromise, annexation of Texas, Mexican Cession, Wilmot Proviso, Compromise of 1850, and Kansas-Nebraska Act"}, {"id": "f54252eb-6efd-426c-a6e9-49dcc1aabb3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular sovereignty, free soil, slave power, and racialized visions of the West"}, {"id": "1154837d-f08d-4ffa-b1c8-74df8630a4a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Bleeding Kansas, border violence, and party realignment"}], "description": "Students trace why western expansion intensified conflict over slavery."}, {"id": "ee3f73a7-7421-408b-972d-9f6734b10549", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political breakdown", "children": [{"id": "512a8f8d-0b61-44ff-bd93-e4dfbdb09357", "type": "concept", "title": "Whigs, Democrats, Free Soil Party, Republicans, Know-Nothings, and southern fire-eaters"}, {"id": "a14e7849-9c49-4eb0-acb1-5dcf510e9ecd", "type": "concept", "title": "Dred Scott, Lincoln-Douglas debates, John Brown, election of 1860, and secession"}, {"id": "062d1be4-0802-4a03-801f-c4116cf3fecf", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional interpretation, federalism, minority rights, and legitimacy"}], "description": "The gist is to explain how institutions failed to contain the crisis."}, {"id": "62815385-936d-4c1c-8f21-870e7f33f050", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Causation and contingency", "children": [{"id": "6941d26d-9b7f-477e-bca2-032966c56599", "type": "concept", "title": "Slavery, capitalism, ideology, honor, race, religion, party politics, and expansion"}, {"id": "9821202f-a8c8-4963-a9ce-09bf4f7a80e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Short-term triggers versus long-term structures"}, {"id": "11d7315a-72ba-4b06-83f2-f1d8ea9bb50c", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterfactual caution, evidence weighting, and historiographical debate"}], "description": "Students practice historical explanation without reducing the crisis to one event."}]}, {"id": "65b0ce80-c83e-4a13-979c-f082ddf4a089", "type": "topic", "title": "Civil War: Military Conflict, Home Fronts, and Transformative War", "description": "Students analyze the Civil War as battlefield conflict, social revolution, political crisis, and lived experience.", "children": [{"id": "b3ab2c28-d890-4c0b-a615-ac749c3b60dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "War aims and strategy", "children": [{"id": "fe931ff9-c4e4-41a2-b47b-bc1ff710b938", "type": "concept", "title": "Union, Confederacy, border states, emancipation, preservation, independence, and nationalism"}, {"id": "7ca0e925-dbbb-4c8a-815e-e4818b52969b", "type": "concept", "title": "Anaconda Plan, eastern and western theaters, naval war, guerrilla warfare, logistics, and technology"}, {"id": "f3306b09-4f65-4443-a76c-98d88122f502", "type": "concept", "title": "Generals, soldiers, leadership, discipline, casualties, disease, and morale"}], "description": "Students analyze changing goals and military realities."}, {"id": "c1ccfc47-a6e4-4f48-b9ad-2e84ff61de5c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Home fronts", "children": [{"id": "b8d184d5-773c-4a95-8561-24fb698de2be", "type": "concept", "title": "Women, enslaved people, free Black communities, immigrants, workers, farmers, children, and refugees"}, {"id": "235adf38-77ea-4971-a417-26da7522940b", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscription, taxation, inflation, shortages, dissent, newspapers, and relief work"}, {"id": "4b83c351-0db5-4a36-aa42-3cd7c6bff05c", "type": "concept", "title": "Confederate nationalism, Union nationalism, class conflict, and civil liberties"}], "description": "The point is to study war as a whole-society event."}, {"id": "a5016afd-a241-4c7d-8588-91adb1674a79", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Transformative war", "children": [{"id": "7b3eb665-855a-40d4-b8bc-f0b958f4d1af", "type": "concept", "title": "Confiscation Acts, Emancipation Proclamation, United States Colored Troops, and Black military service"}, {"id": "ae8bf40e-ae56-4a25-b9af-e46c34acaf42", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal state expansion, railroads, finance, agriculture, and wartime legislation"}, {"id": "a1492e11-409c-4dfe-81d4-7e3afede4ae2", "type": "concept", "title": "Violence, trauma, death, memory, and the meaning of sacrifice"}], "description": "Students examine how the war remade power and freedom."}]}, {"id": "d2384d53-53e0-40f8-8fb0-690ec90edb06", "type": "topic", "title": "Emancipation, Reconstruction, and the Remaking of the Republic", "description": "This module examines emancipation and Reconstruction as a struggle over labor, citizenship, federal power, violence, and democracy.", "children": [{"id": "6509a2b5-3bab-4715-a587-a2eb089e2458", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emancipation process", "children": [{"id": "fc03016c-e43d-4bf2-83b2-f30a2f42c69b", "type": "concept", "title": "Self-emancipation, contraband camps, military policy, Emancipation Proclamation, Thirteenth Amendment, and Black enlistment"}, {"id": "d7685591-aa83-4c01-8a62-92fcb45210e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Family reunification, education, churches, land, labor, and mobility"}, {"id": "bf4a1a17-0593-4802-a9a8-1470ada29d07", "type": "concept", "title": "Freedom's meanings and limits across regions"}], "description": "Students learn emancipation as a struggle, not a single document."}, {"id": "41898ab8-f21a-45ee-8c9d-1659d09ef155", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reconstruction politics", "children": [{"id": "da6c9901-8432-4e01-9b5e-da54bbd924c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Presidential Reconstruction, Congressional Reconstruction, Freedmen's Bureau, Civil Rights Act, Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments"}, {"id": "063e1b65-0230-4dfc-b37c-5cab791664f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Black officeholding, constitutional conventions, public schools, labor contracts, and land debates"}, {"id": "19704db0-c779-4d1a-a0c5-fb5f3f6d0929", "type": "concept", "title": "Republicans, Democrats, Unionists, planters, freedpeople, and federal troops"}], "description": "The gist is to study democracy's expansion and backlash."}, {"id": "6d00d376-61a5-47b5-8ec3-d2b3626a1344", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Violence and retreat", "children": [{"id": "ece84718-0efe-4049-b5d2-ebce599da113", "type": "concept", "title": "Black Codes, Ku Klux Klan, paramilitary violence, redemption, election violence, and federal enforcement"}, {"id": "219b824b-3ef9-4f16-a269-d341b4644538", "type": "concept", "title": "Sharecropping, debt, convict leasing, segregation, and disfranchisement foundations"}, {"id": "15efd3fa-3139-418a-921e-8d191b4a921c", "type": "concept", "title": "Compromise of 1877, memory, Lost Cause, and Reconstruction historiography"}], "description": "Students analyze why Reconstruction's promises were undermined."}]}, {"id": "77f1e3f8-28b3-4bc4-8856-6c8fd116f539", "type": "topic", "title": "Research Capstone: Primary Sources, Historiography, and Public Memory to 1877", "description": "Students synthesize course skills in a source-based project connecting early U.S. history to historiography and public memory.", "children": [{"id": "ca42ccc1-4860-4d99-827d-bdb320c37a92", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research design", "children": [{"id": "1aa7fb1d-e591-4f99-85d5-736b3b8efe75", "type": "concept", "title": "Topic narrowing, question formation, source base, archive plan, and bibliography"}, {"id": "0c7953f2-37ef-47fe-a9e1-85d80cbcb419", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary/secondary source balance, historiographical stakes, and evidence criteria"}, {"id": "9b3bebff-067a-407b-b7e5-fadb499ce7cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Feasibility, scope, timeline, and feedback milestones"}], "description": "Students learn to turn curiosity into an answerable historical question."}, {"id": "b0529aac-b4a6-4bb0-9676-f856ebcc041a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Source-based argument", "children": [{"id": "55c9ab73-98b0-48fa-943f-439c0a1fc6f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Close reading, contextualization, corroboration, source limitations, and counterevidence"}, {"id": "d593bc2e-1268-4ad6-bc24-93ec6abf3841", "type": "concept", "title": "Maps, newspapers, letters, legal records, speeches, images, data, and material culture"}, {"id": "6c7fbe1c-4749-4c3a-80b2-7f92dd8246fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Chicago-style citation, quotations, paraphrase, and ethical source use"}], "description": "The point is to produce a defensible interpretation."}, {"id": "acc9add6-aeca-4789-a67d-83b09dd0ce96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public memory", "children": [{"id": "e5b4f2ba-05ef-4104-8376-b5c5b9fdf0ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, museums, textbooks, documentaries, commemorations, genealogy, and digital exhibits"}, {"id": "168aa85f-c611-44da-bb61-858d593faebc", "type": "concept", "title": "Founding memory, slavery memory, Civil War memory, Reconstruction memory, and Indigenous memory"}, {"id": "e0f6e42d-109f-47a1-b2ff-e41442d714a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-history artifact, research presentation, or digital mini-project"}], "description": "Students connect early U.S. history to how people remember it today."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$ush1json$::jsonb,
